  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">A persisted message (send AND capture) writes one `sprk_communicationparticipant` row per (message × person/address × role {From,To,Cc,Bcc}) with the message as the required `sprk_communication` parent (message grain).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">A resolved person's row sets EXACTLY ONE of `sprk_systemuser`/`sprk_contact`, `sprk_isresolved=true`, and retains `sprk_addresstext` as provenance; resolution reuses `ParticipantCorrelationRung` (`QueryContactByEmailAsync`) — no second resolver.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">An unresolved external address writes a row with BOTH person lookups null, `sprk_isresolved=false`, and `sprk_addresstext` = the raw email (Q-D) — never silently skipped.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">NEGATIVE: no row ever sets both `sprk_systemuser` and `sprk_contact`.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">BEST-EFFORT: a junction-write exception does NOT fail an outbound send and does NOT drop an inbound-captured message — the `sprk_communication` record still persists (NFR-02 discipline).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">IDEMPOTENT: re-processing the same message (inbound echo/retry) creates NO duplicate rows.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Registration is symmetric/unconditional (ADR-032 Null-Object if gated); no `new` credential (ADR-028); existing email/messaging send + capture characterization tests stay green.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">`dotnet build` clean; publish-size delta reported vs ~46.99 MB (expected ≈0); no new HIGH CVE; `/conflict-check` run before the PR.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

